{"ts": 1789778603.27759, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Everything is consistent and reproducible (the repeat of [15,14,18] returned exactly 1.094 \u2014 evaluation is deterministic, so differences between specs are real differences, not sampling jitter).\n\nI'll go with **[15, 14, 18], all long**, in-sample Sharpe 1.094.\n\n**Calibrating my OOS prediction.** The key question is how much of 1.094 is selection bias. Decomposing:\n\n- Feature 15 (~1.005) was picked as the max of 40 roughly-independent draws. But it beat the runner-up by ~0.55 \u2014 far outside the selection-noise band. Its true strength is close to its measured value; shrinkage here is small, maybe 0.02\u20130.05.\n- Features 14/18/19 were *not* selected on their individual marginal values. They were validated structurally \u2014 the \u221a2 and \u221a3 quadrature scaling across pairs and the triple is a prediction that noise cannot fake. That independent confirmation is why I trust them despite weak singles.\n- Choosing 14+18 over 19 from three ~equivalent options costs little: the three triples spanned 1.078\u20131.094, so picking the max of near-identical alternatives inflates by only ~0.01\u20130.015.\n\nTotal selection inflation: modest, ~0.05\u20130.10. That puts my central estimate around **1.00**.\n\nFor the SD, I combine: pure estimation error at n=5000 (~0.22 on an annualized Sharpe), residual uncertainty about how much I've overfit, and the real possibility of regime drift OOS \u2014 signal decay is the norm, not the exception, and the weak 0.44 components are the most fragile. I'll use **0.28**, which leaves sensible probability on the downside (~15% chance of falling below 0.7) without ruling out that it holds near 1.1."}
